<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- One station's work cycle.

     Recovery is expressed, not implied. The Fallback below is what makes a
     failed pick a decision rather than an exception: the station returns home
     and reports itself blocked, so the line attributes the failure here instead
     of discovering it downstream.

     Recovery only works because a leaf that gives up cancels the goal it
     abandons — see SkillNode::send. Without that, MoveToHome below is sent to a
     server that is still executing the goal that just failed, and the skill
     server rejects it: the recovery branch would fail every time, for a reason
     that appears nowhere in this file.

     Nothing in this tree plans a trajectory or commands a controller. Every leaf
     is an L3 skill called as a ROS 2 action — that separation is what lets an
     arm be swapped without touching orchestration (P9).

     Every name below is a blackboard reference. Neither this file nor the
     coordinator writes an asset name or an action name: they arrive as
     parameters, because names are generated from the L0 model and no asset name
     is written by hand twice (CLAUDE.md §8).

     The cycle is pick-then-place, which is one station's whole contribution to
     the line. Transfer — the two-party handoff — is L4's protocol and arrives
     with the second station (ADR-0024). -->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="StationCycle">

  <BehaviorTree ID="StationCycle">
    <Fallback name="cycle_with_recovery">

      <Sequence name="nominal">
        <MoveToHome asset="{asset}" action="{move_to_action}" />
        <PickAt     asset="{asset}" action="{pick_action}"  frame="{pick_frame}"
                    workpiece="{workpiece}" />
        <PlaceAt    asset="{asset}" action="{place_action}" frame="{place_frame}" />
        <MoveToHome asset="{asset}" action="{move_to_action}" />
      </Sequence>

      <!-- NOTHING MOVES HERE (ADR-0037). This branch used to return the arm
           home first, on the reasoning that a station which failed mid-cycle may
           have left the arm somewhere the next attempt would collide with. That
           reasoning is sound and it belongs to a RETRY, and this tree has none:
           there is no `<Repeat>` above, so the recover branch reports and the
           cycle ends. Clearing the arm for an attempt that will not happen is an
           unattended motion after an undiagnosed failure, bought for nothing.

           The nominal branch above already begins with `MoveToHome`, so a cycle
           that is run again clears the arm as its first act — which is the
           correct place for it, inside the attempt rather than before the
           decision to allow one.

           Unlike `line_station.xml` this tree consults no recovery policy at all;
           `ReportBlocked` logs and returns SUCCESS. That gap is not closed here —
           this tree is the single-station coordinator ADR-0024 keeps for testing
           a handoff with one arm, and giving it a policy would be inventing a
           second one. What is fixed is the motion. -->
      <Sequence name="recover">
        <ReportBlocked asset="{asset}" reason="the station could not complete its cycle" />
      </Sequence>

    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
